{% set bc = buildComparison %}
<div id="top"></div>

<section class="lp-hero">
  <div class="lp-wrap lp-hero__grid">
    <div class="lp-hero__copy">
      <p class="lp-eyebrow">Why Java needs a new build system</p>
      <h1>You already wrote <span class="lp-accent">the build script.</span></h1>
      <p class="lp-lede">
        Java builds have grown verbose. A <code>pom.xml</code>, a <code>build.gradle.kts</code> or a
        <code>BUILD.bazel</code> restates what the code already says - its name, its dependencies, its main class -
        and grows a block of configuration for every step beyond the defaults, until the build is a second program
        to maintain beside the first. Jenesis reads the build from the code instead: <code>module-info.java</code>
        names what the project requires, and its Javadoc carries any metadata. There is nothing else to write,
        and no build tool to download.
      </p>
      <p class="lp-lede">
        Not moving to modules yet? Jenesis builds the <code>pom.xml</code> you already have, on the class path as
        Maven does, and the speed and the checks come with it: <code>pin</code> records the version and SHA-256
        checksum of every dependency in a comment of the <code>pom.xml</code>, which Maven ignores, and a build
        that downloads other bytes fails. Every build timed below is built from its <code>pom.xml</code>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <figure class="lp-terminal" aria-label="A Jenesis project and its build command" data-tabs>
      <div class="lp-terminal__bar">
        <span aria-hidden="true"></span><span aria-hidden="true"></span><span aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <div class="lp-terminal__tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="What the build reads">
          <button role="tab" id="tab-hero-module" aria-controls="panel-hero-module" aria-selected="true">module-info.java</button>
          <button role="tab" id="tab-hero-pom" aria-controls="panel-hero-pom" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">pom.xml</button>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="lp-terminal__panels">
        <pre role="tabpanel" id="panel-hero-module" aria-labelledby="tab-hero-module"><code><span class="lp-prompt">$</span> curl -fsSL https://get.jenesis.build | bash
<span class="lp-prompt">$</span> cat sources/module-info.java
<span class="lp-dim">/**
 * @jenesis.release 25
 * @jenesis.main demo.app.Main
 */</span>
module demo.app {
    requires org.slf4j;
    requires com.fasterxml.jackson.databind;
    requires info.picocli;
    requires static org.jspecify;
}
<span class="lp-prompt">$</span> <span class="lp-strong">java build/jenesis/Make.java</span></code></pre>
        <pre role="tabpanel" id="panel-hero-pom" aria-labelledby="tab-hero-pom" hidden><code><span class="lp-prompt">$</span> curl -fsSL https://get.jenesis.build | bash
<span class="lp-prompt">$</span> cat pom.xml
&lt;project&gt;
    &lt;modelVersion&gt;4.0.0&lt;/modelVersion&gt;
    &lt;groupId&gt;demo&lt;/groupId&gt;
    &lt;artifactId&gt;app&lt;/artifactId&gt;
    &lt;version&gt;1.0.0&lt;/version&gt;
    &lt;dependencies&gt;
        &lt;dependency&gt;
            &lt;groupId&gt;com.fasterxml.jackson.core&lt;/groupId&gt;
            &lt;artifactId&gt;jackson-databind&lt;/artifactId&gt;
            &lt;version&gt;2.20.0&lt;/version&gt;
        &lt;/dependency&gt;
    &lt;/dependencies&gt;
&lt;/project&gt;
<span class="lp-prompt">$</span> <span class="lp-strong">java build/jenesis/Make.java</span></code></pre>
      </div>
    </figure>

    <div class="lp-agent" aria-labelledby="agent-label">
      <p class="lp-agent__label" id="agent-label">Moving a build with a coding agent? Hand it this prompt, or start one with it from the root of the project.</p>
      <div class="lp-agent__row">
        <span class="lp-agent__name">Prompt</span>
        <code id="agent-prompt">Run `curl -fsSL https://get.jenesis.build | bash` in the root of this project, then run `java build/jenesis/Make.java prompt/migrate` and carry out the task it prints.</code>
        <button class="lp-button lp-button--small" type="button" data-copy="agent-prompt" aria-describedby="agent-label">Copy</button>
      </div>
      <div class="lp-agent__row">
        <span class="lp-agent__name">Claude Code</span>
        <code id="agent-claude" class="lp-agent__command">claude 'Run `curl -fsSL https://get.jenesis.build | bash` in the root of this project, then run `java build/jenesis/Make.java prompt/migrate` and carry out the task it prints.'</code>
        <button class="lp-button lp-button--small" type="button" data-copy="agent-claude" aria-describedby="agent-label">Copy</button>
      </div>
      <div class="lp-agent__row">
        <span class="lp-agent__name">Codex</span>
        <code id="agent-codex" class="lp-agent__command">codex 'Run `curl -fsSL https://get.jenesis.build | bash` in the root of this project, then run `java build/jenesis/Make.java prompt/migrate` and carry out the task it prints.'</code>
        <button class="lp-button lp-button--small" type="button" data-copy="agent-codex" aria-describedby="agent-label">Copy</button>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="lp-results" aria-labelledby="migrations">
      <h2 class="lp-results__title" id="migrations">Builds an agent has moved</h2>
      <p class="lp-small">A coding agent moved each of these open-source builds, starting from the prompt above and
        nothing else; agent time is the whole migration, from reading the build to comparing jars, tests and POMs
        until they matched. Each cell shows how much less time Jenesis took than the original build doing the same work,
        above the two times.</p>
      <div class="lp-tabs" data-tabs>
        <div class="lp-tabs__list" role="tablist" aria-label="Build times">
          {%- for p in migrations.pairings %}
          <button role="tab" id="tab-{{ p.id }}" aria-controls="panel-{{ p.id }}" aria-selected="{{ 'true' if loop.first else 'false' }}"{% if not loop.first %} tabindex="-1"{% endif %}>{{ p.label }}</button>
          {%- endfor %}
        </div>
        {%- for p in migrations.pairings %}
        <div class="lp-tabs__panel" role="tabpanel" id="panel-{{ p.id }}" aria-labelledby="tab-{{ p.id }}"{% if not loop.first %} hidden{% endif %}>
          <table class="lp-matrix">
            <colgroup><col class="lp-results__project"><col class="lp-results__tool"><col class="lp-results__agent"><col class="lp-results__full"><col class="lp-results__again"><col class="lp-results__edit"></colgroup>
            <thead><tr><th scope="col">Project</th><th scope="col">Built with</th><th scope="col">Agent time</th><th scope="col">Full build<br>Jenesis / before</th><th scope="col">Nothing changed<br>Jenesis / before</th><th scope="col">One method edited<br>Jenesis / before</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {%- for r in p.rows %}
              <tr><th scope="row"><a class="lp-link" href="{{ r.repository }}">{{ r.name }}</a></th><td>{{ r.tool }}</td><td>{{ r.agent }}</td>{% for c in r.cells %}<td><span class="lp-speedup{% if c.spread %} lp-speedup--spread{% endif %}">{{ c.saving }}</span><span class="lp-times">{{ c.times }}</span></td>{% endfor %}</tr>
              {%- endfor %}
            </tbody>
            <tfoot>
              <tr><th scope="row" colspan="3">Median saving</th>{% for m in p.medians %}<td><span class="lp-speedup">{{ m }}</span></td>{% endfor %}</tr>
            </tfoot>
          </table>
        </div>
        {%- endfor %}
      </div>
      <p class="lp-small lp-results__method">Every build ran alone on a four-core machine, and every time is the
        median of three runs, except Guava's without and with a daemon, whose builds take more than five minutes and
        ran once. Without a
        daemon, Jenesis ran under <code>jenesis.make.mode=compiled</code>, Maven as plain <code>mvn</code> and Gradle with <code>--no-daemon</code>; with one, Jenesis under
        <code>jenesis.make.mode=daemon</code>, Maven as <code>mvnd</code> and Gradle with <code>--daemon</code>,
        each warmed up by a first build. From an ahead-of-time cache, Jenesis ran under
        <code>jenesis.make.mode=aot</code> from its compiled classes, with every cache trained by that first build,
        as a CI server restores them, and is set beside the original build without a daemon. Runs of one build differed by about 5&nbsp;%, so a saving within that
        spread is set in grey. After an edit, each build compiled the edited module again and ran the tests of every
        module that depends on it; each edit was a new one, so no build cache held its result. Every build stayed on
        <code>pom.xml</code>, and the two builds ran the same tests, except those Jenesis leaves to the old build:
        AssertJ's 31 and Gson's 12 tests that rely on the Java Module System, and in Guava, Mockito and JUnit 5 the
        test classes outside Jenesis' default naming, under 1&nbsp;% of their tests, beside 39 tests the JUnit 5
        migration leaves out. <code>mvnd</code> builds AssertJ only once its Spring Boot tests declare
        <code>assertj-core</code>, which its parallel reactor otherwise starts too early. Each migration's patch and
        the script that measured it are <a class="lp-link" href="https://github.com/jenesis/jenesis-documentation/tree/main/benchmarks">on GitHub</a>,
        to run again on a machine of your own. Measuring builds this way is imperfect - one machine, a few runs and
        builds an agent moved - but the figures still show the general tendency: Jenesis was at least as fast as the
        original build in every case, from about 58,000 lines of Java with no dependencies, against about 160,000 for
        Maven 3.9's core and resolver before any plugin and over a million for Gradle.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

