<section class="lp-band" id="migrate" aria-labelledby="migrate-title">
  <div class="lp-wrap">
    <h2 id="migrate-title">How to migrate to Jenesis?</h2>
    <p>Hand it to a coding agent. A migration is work a build tool checks automatically, so an agent can move one
      concern at a time and build after each step, and every failure has a single cause it can fix. Jenesis ships
      the briefing for this: <code>skill/migrate</code> walks through the move from a Maven or Gradle build, and
      <code>skill/registry</code> names what replaces each plugin. With it, an agent is normally able to port a build
      autonomously and at a low cost. The skills for this are developed by having an agent try exactly this with a
      range of open-source builds.</p>
    <div class="lp-command"><pre><code>java build/jenesis/Make.java skill/migrate</code></pre></div>
    <p>The most common Maven and Gradle plugins - compilers, code generators, checks, test runners and coverage -
      have a counterpart: a tool the JDK ships, run directly, or an official plugin the build fetches where a
      configuration file switches it on. A less common or custom plugin is ported to one of the
      <a class="lp-link" href="{{ docs }}extending-the-build/#hook-points">hook points</a> of a plugin, without
      changing anything else of the build. The result is validated by comparing what both builds produce: the
      jar contents, the dependency tree, the number of tests run and the POM a consumer receives.</p>
    <p>The builds an agent has moved this way, and how long each build takes before and after, are
      <a class="lp-link" href="#migrations">at the top of this page</a>.</p>
    <p class="lp-after">
      <a class="lp-link" href="{{ docs }}migrating/">Migrating a Maven or Gradle build, step by step &rarr;</a>
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="lp-band lp-band--tint" id="faq" aria-labelledby="faq-title">
  <div class="lp-wrap lp-faq">
    <h2 id="faq-title">Questions</h2>
    <details>
      <summary>Does my project have to use the Java Module System?</summary>
      <p>No. Jenesis reads a <code>pom.xml</code> as the description of what to build, which is the quickest way to
        try it on a project you already have. Where a project declares no modules, its jars resolve by coordinate
        and compile on the class path.</p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>What if a convention does not cover my build?</summary>
      <p>The extension is a small Java function or module describing one step, launched by the JDK like the rest
        of the build - with types, an IDE and refactoring, the same as your application.</p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>How were the examples on this page made?</summary>
      <p>Each one is a project that was built and run with its tool, and the files shown are
        copied from it unchanged. The versions are named <a href="#examples">before the first example</a>.</p>
    </details>
  </div>
</section>

